            <div xml:id="d374">

                <pb facs="0458" n="448" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg448"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.506/48: Airgram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-09-27T14:00:00+07:00">September 27,
                            1943—2 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 13—3 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A–65. Department’s 889, July 12, 5 p.m. and Embassy’s A–21, July 31, 1 p.m. A
                    responsible official of the Finance Ministry now <pb facs="0459" n="449"
                        xml:id="pg449"/> states that the insurance project has been set aside at
                    least temporarily, due to fear that it might be regarded as a step in the
                    direction of Government monopoly. He says that if it should again be brought up
                    for consideration, it will be in such form as to cause no concern to foreign
                    capital; and that the Government is conscious of need for foreign capital and is
                    aware that investors are timid and must be reassured by actions as well as by
                    laws.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
